{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 3144 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "91baec175c827e8c5aad0aadd83acd598d8d223b80f7304182a13203921fd434" :edb/db-before-t 3143 :edb/db-after-t 3144 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536456 50 #inst "2026-10-17T01:19:50.695Z" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186059691 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573872 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186059691 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573941 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186059692 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573918 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186059693 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573899 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186059930 1050 420.0 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186060112 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186060413 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186060415 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186060422 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186060985 1064 "A conditional statement $A \\Rightarrow B$ has three related logical forms:\n\n- the **converse:** $B \\Rightarrow A$\n\n- the **inverse:** $\\lnot A \\Rightarrow \\lnot B$\n\n- the **contrapositive:** $\\lnot B \\Rightarrow \\lnot A$\n\nFor example, consider the following true statement:\n\n$\\qquad$ If I am George Washington, then I am an American.\n\nHere, the antecedent is $A=$ \"I am George Washington,\" and the consequent is $B=$ \"I am an American.\"\n\n- The *converse* is \"If I am an American, then I am George Washington.\"\n\n- The *inverse* is \"If I am not George Washington, then I am not an American.\"\n\n- The *contrapositive* is \"If I am not an American, then I am not George Washington.\"\n\nAs we can see from the statements above, only the contrapositive is also true." 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186060985 1064 "A conditional statement $A \\Rightarrow B$ has three related logical forms:\n\n- the **converse:** $B \\Rightarrow A$\n- the **inverse:** $\\lnot A \\Rightarrow \\lnot B$\n- the **contrapositive:** $\\lnot B \\Rightarrow \\lnot A$\n\nFor example, consider the following true statement:\n\n$\\qquad$ If I am George Washington, then I am an American.\n\nHere, the antecedent is $A=$ \"I am George Washington,\" and the consequent is $B=$ \"I am an American.\"\n\n- The *converse* is \"If I am an American, then I am George Washington.\"\n- The *inverse* is \"If I am not George Washington, then I am not an American.\"\n- The *contrapositive* is \"If I am not an American, then I am not George Washington.\"\n\nAs we can see from the statements above, only the contrapositive is also true." 13194139536456 17592186045417 false] [17592186060986 1064 "In general, we have the following facts:\n\n- The contrapositive is logically equivalent to the original statement:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{A \\Rightarrow B}_{\\textrm{original}} \\equiv \\underbrace{\\neg B \\Rightarrow \\neg A}_{\\textrm{contrapositive}}\n$$\n\n- The inverse and the converse are logically equivalent to each other but not to the original statement:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\neg A \\Rightarrow \\neg B}_{\\textrm{inverse}} \\equiv \\underbrace{B \\Rightarrow A}_{\\textrm{converse}}\n$$\n\nWe can prove these results using a truth table, as shown below:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, A \\Rightarrow B \\,$ | $\\, \\neg A \\Rightarrow \\neg B \\,$ | $\\, B \\Rightarrow A \\,$ | $\\, \\neg B \\Rightarrow \\neg A \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{red} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{red} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{red} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{red} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{red} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{red} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{red} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{red} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n\nThis can be visualized using the following diagram.\n\n![](images/d9/d946d2a81d46cbde934ebbab54f9b69b514092e02da4f8a3b79768f218929449.png)" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186060986 1064 "In general, we have the following facts:\n\n- The contrapositive is logically equivalent to the original statement: $$\\underbrace{A \\Rightarrow B}_{\\textrm{original}} \\equiv \\underbrace{\\neg B \\Rightarrow \\neg A}_{\\textrm{contrapositive}}$$\n- The inverse and the converse are logically equivalent to each other but not to the original statement: $$\\underbrace{\\neg A \\Rightarrow \\neg B}_{\\textrm{inverse}} \\equiv \\underbrace{B \\Rightarrow A}_{\\textrm{converse}}$$\n\nWe can prove these results using a truth table, as shown below:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, A \\Rightarrow B \\,$ | $\\, \\neg A \\Rightarrow \\neg B \\,$ | $\\, B \\Rightarrow A \\,$ | $\\, \\neg B \\Rightarrow \\neg A \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{red} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{red} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{red} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{red} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{red} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{red} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{red} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{red} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n\nThis can be visualized using the following diagram.\n\n![](images/d9/d946d2a81d46cbde934ebbab54f9b69b514092e02da4f8a3b79768f218929449.png)" 13194139536456 17592186045417 false] [17592186064171 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573895 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186064172 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573944 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186064177 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573873 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186064408 1050 420.0 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186064889 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186065408 1064 "Remember that if $a$ is a positive real number, then the derivative of $a^x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm d x}\\left( a^ x\\right)\t=\ta^ x \\ln a.\n$$\n\nSo, by \"reversing\" this process, we obtain the following formula for the integral of a general exponential function:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int  a^x \\, \\textrm d x  = \\dfrac{a^ x }{\\ln a} +C.\n$$\n\nFor instance, suppose that we wish to calculate the integral \n\n$$\n\\int 2^x  \\, \\textrm d x\n$$\n\nTo compute the integral of a general exponential function, we just need to divide by the natural logarithm of the base of the exponential function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 2^x  \\, \\textrm d x   &= \\dfrac{2^x}{\\ln 2} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186065408 1064 "Remember that if $a$ is a positive real number, then the derivative of $a^x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm d x}\\left( a^ x\\right)\t=\ta^ x \\ln a.\n$$\n\nSo, by \"reversing\" this process, we obtain the following formula for the integral of a general exponential function:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int  a^x \\, \\textrm d x  = \\dfrac{a^ x }{\\ln a} +C.\n$$\n\nFor instance, suppose that we wish to calculate the integral \n\n$$\n\\int 2^x  \\, \\textrm d x\n$$\n\nTo compute the integral of a general exponential function, we just need to divide by the natural logarithm of the base of the exponential function:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\int 2^x  \\, \\textrm d x   &= \\dfrac{2^x}{\\ln 2} + C\n}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 false] [17592186083387 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573917 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186092360 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573907 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186092360 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573912 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186092360 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573942 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186092361 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573887 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186092361 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573906 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186092361 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573915 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186092361 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573940 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186092362 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573875 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186092362 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573885 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186092362 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573935 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186092362 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573936 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186092398 1050 1020.0 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186093400 1064 "Consider the following geometric sequence: \n\n$$\n3,\\quad 6,\\quad 12,\\quad 24, \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\n \n\nSuppose we want to find the first term that exceeds $3\\,000\\,000.$ We can do this by creating and solving an inequality.\n\nFirst, let's construct a formula for the $n$th term of the sequence. The first term of the sequence is $a_1=3,$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac 6 3 = 2.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $3\\,000\\,000.$ So, we can set up and solve the corresponding inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & >3\\,000\\,000 \\\\ 3\\cdot {2}^{n-1} & >3\\,000\\,000 \\\\ {2}^{n-1} & >1\\,000\\,000 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}({2}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}(1\\,000\\,000) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}(2) & >\\operatorname{log}(1\\,000\\,000) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}(1\\,000\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}(2)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}(1\\,000\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}(2)}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\log (1\\,000\\,000)} {\\log (2)} + 1 \\approx 20.932.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $21$st term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $3\\,000\\,000.$\n\nTo check our answer, we can easily verify that $a_{20} < 3\\, 000\\, 000$ and $a_{21} > 3\\, 000\\, 000\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{20} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{20-1}=3\\cdot {2}^{19}=1\\,572\\,864<3\\,000\\,000 \\\\ {a}_{21} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{21-1}=3\\cdot {2}^{20}=3\\,145\\,728>3\\,000\\,000\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $a_{21}$ is indeed the first term that exceeds $3\\,000\\,000.$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 false] [17592186093400 1064 "Consider the following geometric sequence: \n\n$$\n3,\\quad 6,\\quad 12,\\quad 24, \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\n \n\nSuppose we want to find the first term that exceeds $3\\,000\\,000.$ We can do this by creating and solving an inequality.\n\nFirst, let's construct a formula for the $n$th term of the sequence. The first term of the sequence is $a_1=3,$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac 6 3 = 2.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $3\\,000\\,000.$ So, we can set up and solve the corresponding inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & >3\\,000\\,000 \\\\ 3\\cdot {2}^{n-1} & >3\\,000\\,000 \\\\ {2}^{n-1} & >1\\,000\\,000 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({2}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(2) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\log (1\\,000\\,000)} {\\log (2)} + 1 \\approx 20.932.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $21$st term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $3\\,000\\,000.$\n\nTo check our answer, we can easily verify that $a_{20} < 3\\, 000\\, 000$ and $a_{21} > 3\\, 000\\, 000\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{20} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{20-1}=3\\cdot {2}^{19}=1\\,572\\,864<3\\,000\\,000 \\\\ {a}_{21} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{21-1}=3\\cdot {2}^{20}=3\\,145\\,728>3\\,000\\,000\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $a_{21}$ is indeed the first term that exceeds $3\\,000\\,000.$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186096612 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573897 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186096612 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573948 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186096804 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573874 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186096805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573879 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186096805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573898 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186096805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573920 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186096807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573883 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186096807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573924 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186096990 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186105700 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573868 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186105700 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573934 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186105715 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573896 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186105720 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573909 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186105720 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573922 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186105854 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573882 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186105994 1050 600.0 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186106280 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186107147 1064 "For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C = \\ln K,$ where $K>0$ is some other constant. Then, using the laws of logarithms, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}|x|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}|x|+\\operatorname{ln}K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 false] [17592186107147 1064 "For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C = \\ln K,$ where $K>0$ is some other constant. Then, using the laws of logarithms, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186115190 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573927 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186115190 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573943 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186115191 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573884 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186115192 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573894 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186115193 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573900 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186115321 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573939 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186115323 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573923 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186115457 1050 1320.0 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186115906 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186116473 1064 "The derivatives of the reciprocal trigonometric functions are shown in the table below:\n\n| $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\sec x$ | $\\sec x\\tan x$ |\n| $\\csc x$ | $-\\csc x\\cot x$ |\n| $\\cot x$ | $-\\csc^2 x$ |\n\nAs with the standard trigonometric functions, we can now differentiate any combination of the above functions using the rules of differentiation.\n\nFor example, let's find the derivative of \n\n$$\ny= \\sec x + \\csc x.\n$$\n\nWe can take the derivative of each term and then add them, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}x+\\operatorname{csc}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{csc}x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x-\\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We could have computed the derivative of the above function by rewriting it as\n\n$$\ny= \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x} + \\dfrac{1}{\\sin x}\n$$\n\nand applying the appropriate derivative rules. However, this would have taken significantly more work! You can save a lot of time by memorizing the derivatives of the reciprocal trigonometric functions." 13194139536456 17592186045417 false] [17592186116473 1064 "The derivatives of the reciprocal trigonometric functions are shown in the table below:\n\n| $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\sec x$ | $\\sec x\\tan x$ |\n| $\\csc x$ | $-\\csc x\\cot x$ |\n| $\\cot x$ | $-\\csc^2 x$ |\n\nAs with the standard trigonometric functions, we can now differentiate any combination of the above functions using the rules of differentiation.\n\nFor example, let's find the derivative of \n\n$$\ny= \\sec x + \\csc x.\n$$\n\nWe can take the derivative of each term and then add them, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\operatorname{csc}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We could have computed the derivative of the above function by rewriting it as\n\n$$\ny= \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x} + \\dfrac{1}{\\sin x}\n$$\n\nand applying the appropriate derivative rules. However, this would have taken significantly more work! You can save a lot of time by memorizing the derivatives of the reciprocal trigonometric functions." 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186116474 1064 "To see why the derivative of $\\sec x$ is $\\sec x \\tan x,$ we start by remembering that\n\n$$\n\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}.\n$$\n\nIf we now differentiate the above relation using the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}x-1\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}x)}{{[\\operatorname{cos}x]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}x-1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}x)}{{cos}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{{cos}^{2}x} \\\\  & ={{\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}x}}_{⏟}}_{\\operatorname{sec}x}{{\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x}}_{⏟}}_{\\operatorname{tan}x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the same method to find the derivative of the other two reciprocal trigonometric functions,\n\n$$\n\\csc x = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin x} \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \\cot x = \\dfrac{1}{\\tan x}.\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 false] [17592186116474 1064 "To see why the derivative of $\\sec x$ is $\\sec x \\tan x,$ we start by remembering that\n\n$$\n\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}.\n$$\n\nIf we now differentiate the above relation using the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & ={{\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}_{⏟}}_{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}{{\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}_{⏟}}_{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the same method to find the derivative of the other two reciprocal trigonometric functions,\n\n$$\n\\csc x = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin x} \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \\cot x = \\dfrac{1}{\\tan x}.\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186116524 1064 "Trigonometric functions appear very frequently in calculus, so it's extremely important to commit their derivatives to memory.\n\nConveniently, there are some mnemonic tricks that we can use to more easily remember the derivatives of trigonometric functions.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}x) & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\, & \\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}x) & ={sec}^{2}x\\, & \\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}x) & =\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cot}x) & =-{csc}^{2}x\\, & \\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{csc}x) & =-\\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's helpful to notice that the \"co\"-functions (cosine, cosecant, and cotangent) all have negative signs in their derivatives, while the other trigonometric functions (sine, secant, and tangent) do not.\n\nIt's also helpful to realize that functions come in pairs.\n\n- Sine and cosine are a pair because each appears in the other's derivative.\n\n- Tangent and secant are a pair because each appears in the other's derivative.\n\n- Cotangent and cosecant are a pair because each appears in the other's derivative.\n\nLastly, it's helpful to realize the following:\n\n- Ignoring the positive or negative sign, tangent and cotangent have a similar format for their derivatives: the square of the function they're paired with.\n\n- Ignoring the positive or negative sign, secant and cosecant have a similar format for their derivatives: the product of themself and the function they're paired with." 13194139536456 17592186045417 false] [17592186116524 106