cimal places." 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186124443 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573871 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186124443 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573893 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186124444 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573869 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186124445 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573938 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186124447 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573901 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186124447 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573903 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186124447 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573928 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186124447 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573946 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186124940 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186125726 1064 "The **gradient** of a multivariable function is analogous to the derivative of a single-variable function.\n\nFor a function $f(x,y)$ whose partial derivatives exist at a point $(x,y),$ the **gradient vector** is denoted $\\nabla f(x,y)$ and can be found by computing the vector of partial derivatives:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f (x,y) &=\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} \\mathbf i + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\mathbf j\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186125726 1064 "The **gradient** of a multivariable function is analogous to the derivative of a single-variable function.\n\nFor a function $f(x,y)$ whose partial derivatives exist at a point $(x,y),$ the **gradient vector** is denoted $\\nabla f(x,y)$ and can be found by computing the vector of partial derivatives:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\nabla f (x,y) =  \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} \\mathbf i + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\mathbf j\n                              \n}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 false] [17592186125727 1064 "For a function of three variables $f(x,y,z)$, the gradient of $f$ is given by the vector  \n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla \\! f (x,y,z) &= f_x(x,y,z)\\ \\mathbf i + f_y (x,y,z)\\ \\mathbf j + f_z (x,y,z)\\ \\mathbf k.\n                             \n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFor a function $f(x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n)$ of $n$ variables, its gradient is usually given as a column vector:\n\n![](images/21/21e05d85506da576e293bce86b1ca98f4f5b101c49b0591a49d80d2a4b8bfcc1.svg)" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186125727 1064 "For a function of three variables $f(x,y,z)$, the gradient of $f$ is given by the vector  \n\n \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\nabla \\! f (x,y,z) &= f_x(x,y,z)\\ \\mathbf i + f_y (x,y,z)\\ \\mathbf j + f_z (x,y,z)\\ \\mathbf k.\n                             \n}\n$$\n\n \n\nFor a function $f(x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n)$ of $n$ variables, its gradient is usually given as a column vector:\n\n![](images/21/21e05d85506da576e293bce86b1ca98f4f5b101c49b0591a49d80d2a4b8bfcc1.svg)" 13194139536456 17592186045417 false] [17592186129145 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573890 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186129147 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573876 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186129162 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573945 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186129163 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573908 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186129163 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573913 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186129167 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573902 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186133747 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573929 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186133748 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573867 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186133749 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573892 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186152201 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573878 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186152202 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573905 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186152372 1050 420.0 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186152835 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186152837 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186152838 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186153395 1064 "**De Morgan's laws** for sets tell us alternative ways to represent the complement of a union or intersection.\n\n- De Morgan's law for unions states that\n\n$$\n\\overline{A \\cup B} \\equiv \\overline{A} \\cap \\overline{B}.\n$$\n\n- De Morgan's law for intersections states that\n\n$$\n\\overline{A \\cap B} \\equiv \\overline{A} \\cup \\overline{B}.\n$$\n\nIntuitively, you can think that the complement \"flips\" the union into an intersection and vice versa.\n\nTo demonstrate De Morgan's laws, consider the sets $A = \\{ 1,2,3 \\}$ and $B = \\{ 3,4 \\}$ in the universe $U = \\{ 1,2,3,4,5,6 \\}.$\n\n- Computing $\\overline{A \\cup B},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{A\\cup B} & =\\overset{―}{\\{1,2,3\\}\\cup \\{3,4\\}} \\\\  & =\\overset{―}{\\{1,2,3,4\\}} \\\\  & =\\{5,6\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\overline{A} \\cap \\overline{B},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{A}\\cap \\overset{―}{B} & =\\overset{―}{\\{1,2,3\\}}\\cap \\overset{―}{\\{3,4\\}} \\\\  & =\\{4,5,6\\}\\cap \\{1,2,5,6\\} \\\\  & =\\{5,6\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have that $\\overline{A \\cup B} = \\overline{A} \\cap \\overline{B}. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\n- Computing $\\overline{A \\cap B},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{A\\cap B} & =\\overset{―}{\\{1,2,3\\}\\cap \\{3,4\\}} \\\\  & =\\overset{―}{\\{3\\}} \\\\  & =\\{1,2,4,5,6\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\overline{A} \\cup \\overline{B},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{A}\\cup \\overset{―}{B} & =\\overset{―}{\\{1,2,3\\}}\\cup \\overset{―}{\\{3,4\\}} \\\\  & =\\{4,5,6\\}\\cup \\{1,2,5,6\\} \\\\  & =\\{1,2,4,5,6\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have that $\\overline{A \\cap B} = \\overline{A} \\cup \\overline{B}. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186153395 1064 "**De Morgan's laws** for sets tell us alternative ways to represent the complement of a union or intersection.\n\n- De Morgan's law for unions states that $$\\overline{A \\cup B} \\equiv \\overline{A} \\cap \\overline{B}.$$\n- De Morgan's law for intersections states that $$\\overline{A \\cap B} \\equiv \\overline{A} \\cup \\overline{B}.$$\n\nIntuitively, you can think that the complement \"flips\" the union into an intersection and vice versa.\n\nTo demonstrate De Morgan's laws, consider the sets $A = \\{ 1,2,3 \\}$ and $B = \\{ 3,4 \\}$ in the universe $U = \\{ 1,2,3,4,5,6 \\}.$\n\n- Computing $\\overline{A \\cup B},$ we have $$\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{A\\cup B} & =\\overset{―}{{1,2,3}\\cup {3,4}} \\\\ & =\\overset{―}{{1,2,3,4}} \\\\ & ={5,6}.\\end{aligned}$$ Computing $\\overline{A} \\cap \\overline{B},$ we have $$\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{A}\\cap \\overset{―}{B} & =\\overset{―}{{1,2,3}}\\cap \\overset{―}{{3,4}} \\\\ & ={4,5,6}\\cap {1,2,5,6} \\\\ & ={5,6}.\\end{aligned}$$ So, we have that $\\overline{A \\cup B} = \\overline{A} \\cap \\overline{B}. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n- Computing $\\overline{A \\cap B},$ we have $$\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{A\\cap B} & =\\overset{―}{{1,2,3}\\cap {3,4}} \\\\ & =\\overset{―}{{3}} \\\\ & ={1,2,4,5,6}.\\end{aligned}$$ Computing $\\overline{A} \\cup \\overline{B},$ we have $$\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{A}\\cup \\overset{―}{B} & =\\overset{―}{{1,2,3}}\\cup \\overset{―}{{3,4}} \\\\ & ={4,5,6}\\cup {1,2,5,6} \\\\ & ={1,2,4,5,6}.\\end{aligned}$$ So, we have that $\\overline{A \\cap B} = \\overline{A} \\cup \\overline{B}. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 false] [17592186162007 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573933 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186179684 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573914 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186179684 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573921 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186180302 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186198306 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573877 1319413953