39536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573864 1075 #edb/ref 17592186573436 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573864 1075 #edb/ref 17592186573455 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573864 1075 #edb/ref 17592186573490 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573864 1075 #edb/ref 17592186573506 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573864 1075 #edb/ref 17592186573680 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573864 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573864 1077 "selection" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573864 1078 #edb/ref 17592186573436 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573864 1079 #uuid "fc9d886d-2453-589c-b70a-85755f09affc" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573865 1075 #edb/ref 17592186573663 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573865 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573865 1077 "field-2" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573865 1078 #edb/ref 17592186573663 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573865 1079 #uuid "fe379273-e82c-52d1-918e-5206d2fef928" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573866 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573859 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573866 1069 "q-181729" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573866 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573866 1071 "Complete the following reasoning about the set $S=(-\\infty ,1):$\n\nThe value $1$ is not the maximum of the set $S$ because {{field-1}}." 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573866 1072 #uuid "047835f4-a894-5a97-94f4-46a25873057e" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573866 1074 "Recall that ${x}_{max}$ is the maximum of a set $S$ if \n\n- ${x}_{max}\\in S,$ and\n\n- $x\\le {x}_{max}$ for all $x\\in S.$\n\nIn our case, the value $1$ is not the maximum of the set $S$ because $1\\notin S.$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573867 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573830 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573867 1069 "q-236727" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573867 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573867 1071 "Consider the following arithmetic sequence:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}={a}_{n}-6,\\,\\,{a}_{1}=5,\\,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$\n\nThe explicit formula for the $n$th term is\n\n${a}_{n}=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,n\\ge 1.$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573867 1072 #uuid "0567f346-23f3-5836-b41f-674f2f72a9b2" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573867 1074 "The explicit formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\\,n\\ge 1,\n$$\n\nso we need to determine the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nWe are given that the first term is ${a}_{1}=5,$ and we can use the recursive rule to compute the second term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={a}_{1}-6 \\\\  & =5-6 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the first and second terms, we can compute the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =-1-5 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute ${a}_{1}=5$ and $d=-6$ into the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =5+(n-1)(-6) \\\\  & =5-6n+6 \\\\  & =11-6n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=11-6n,\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573868 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573797 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573868 1069 "q-336962" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573868 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573868 1071 "If $r(x)=-2{x}^{8}$ and $s(x)=\\frac{11}{{x}^{10}}$, then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{2}\\cdot r(x)\\cdot s(x))=$  \n{{field-1}}." 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573868 1072 #uuid "0a3cac7e-5a2a-596f-b2a3-d5e28243a2e3" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573868 1074 "First, let's calculate ${x}^{2}\\cdot r(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\cdot r(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot (-2{x}^{8})=-2{x}^{10}\n$$\n\nWhen we multiply the two functions ${x}^{2}\\cdot r(x)$ and $s(x)$, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{2}\\cdot r(x)\\cdot s(x)) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-2{x}^{10}\\cdot \\frac{11}{{x}^{10}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-22 \\\\  & =-22\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573869 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573845 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573869 1069 "q-127950" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573869 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573869 1071 "Find $∇f(x,y,z),$  given that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{1/2}}.$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573869 1072 #uuid "0d01726f-52af-5592-8eb9-d6abaee05cc0" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573869 1074 "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{3/2}} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{3/2}} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{z}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(-\\frac{x}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{3/2}})i+(-\\frac{y}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{3/2}})j+(-\\frac{z}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{3/2}})k \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{3/2}}(x\\,i+y\\,j+z\\,k)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573870 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573834 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573870 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573858 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573870 1069 "q-181728" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573870 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573870 1071 "What are the minimum and maximum of the set $A=\\{n\\in R∣\\,5<n\\le 9\\}?$\n\nThe minimum of the set {{field-1}}, while the maximum of the set {{field-2}}." 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573870 1072 #uuid "0eb91ec7-8393-5d8f-843e-b9507e995da6" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573870 1074 "Recall that \n\n- ${x}_{max}$ is the maximum of a set $S$ if ${x}_{max}\\in S$ and $x\\le {x}_{max}$ for all $x\\in S,$ and\n\n- ${x}_{min}$ is the minimum of a set $S$ if ${x}_{min}\\in S$ and $x\\ge {x}_{min}$ for all $x\\in S.$\n\nNotice that $A=(5,9],$ where $5\\notin A.$ So, we have the following:\n\n- $min(A)=min(5,9],$ which $\\text{does not exist}$\n\n- $max(A)=min(5,9]=9$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573871 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573831 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573871 1069 "q-127649" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573871 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573871 1071 "If $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}{e}^{x/y},$ find $∇f(x,y).$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573871 1072 #uuid "11d17df4-8621-5dfb-b614-0130a53b1042" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573871 1074 "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{y}{x}{e}^{x/y}) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{y}){e}^{x/y} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-y}{{x}^{2}}{e}^{x/y} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{y}{x}{e}^{x/y}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}){e}^{x/y} \\\\  & =\\frac{y-x}{xy}{e}^{x/y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\text{ }i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{x-y}{{x}^{2}}{e}^{x/y}\\text{ }i+\\frac{y-x}{xy}{e}^{x/y}\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573872 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573828 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573872 1069 "q-183289" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573872 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573872 1071 "Find the converse of the following statement: \n\n$\\,$ **If** $4$ is even, **then** $5$ is odd." 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573872 1072 #uuid "125cbfce-91f0-5374-bd89-78fd1d13de57" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573872 1074 "In our conditional statement:\n\n- The antecedent is $A:$ $4$ is even.\n\n- The consequent is $B:$ $5$ is odd.\n\nThe converse of a conditional statement $A⇒B$ is given by \n\n$$\nB⇒A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the converse is the following: \n\n$\\,$ If $5$ is odd, then $4$ is even." 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573873 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573860 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573873 1069 "q-2279" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573873 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573873 1071 "$\\int ({4}^{x}-{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573873 1072 #uuid "149a14d8-d9d3-538d-9d01-436b9432ab15" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573873 1074 "Here, we combine the sum rule and our exponential rules to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int ({4}^{x}-{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{4}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-{e}^{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573874 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573854 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573874 1069 "q-344119" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573874 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573874 1071 "![](images/63/6337a487598d8b5e432301b1a0d9a64407323a6bdcfa0f2fa7ff2ee140fac1bd.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is given above. Find ${f}^{′}(5).$\n\n${f}^{′}(5)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573874 1072 #uuid "17f4d138-65e0-5f89-8826-9793c7584c9d" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573874 1074 "To compute ${f}^{′}(5),$ we need to find the slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ at $x=5.$\n\nLet's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ for $x\\in [4,6].$ Notice that $x=5$ lies in this interval.\n\n![](images/42/42b4ddb29283047bd7ea8141ee14c793607b6b79a18f5d599a93839aec5a77b5.png)\n\nThe slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in (4,6)$ is constant and equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{5-(-1)}{6-4}=\\frac{6}{2}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(5)=3.$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573875 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573800 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573875 1069 "q-278086" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573875 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573875 1071 "The $3$rd term of a geometric sequence is $18$, and the $6$th term is $486.$ The first term of this sequence to exceed $10\\,000$ is the{{field-1}} th term." 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573875 1072 #uuid "1d00b5b7-e529-5ab1-8ece-1078cbcb48a1" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573875 1074 "Since we are given that ${a}_{3}=18$ and ${a}_{6}=486$, we can find the common ratio $r$ of the sequence, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{n-m} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}} \\\\ {r}^{6-3} & =\\frac{{a}_{6}}{{a}_{3}} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{486}{18} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =27 \\\\ r & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${a}_{3}=18$ and  $r=3$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{3-1} \\\\ 18 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\ 18 & =9{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {3}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $10\\,000.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {3}^{n-1} & >10\\,000 \\\\ {3}^{n-1} & >5\\,000 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({3}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(5\\,000) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(5\\,000) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}+1≈8.753.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $9$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $10\\,000.$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573876 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573771 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573876 1069 "q-80354" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573876 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573876 1071 "![](images/7b/7bcc2f587bff8042ea087d8a3ba3ea8be5d7de513cdc3bd37f2a1cafdb4e278a.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y=-x(x+1)(x+3)^{2}?$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573876 1072 #uuid "1d7f099c-ef0d-57d9-9c76-6ca878f1638e" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573876 1074 "Based on the factored equation, we can make the following conclusions about the graph:\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the roots $x=-1,0$ is $1,$ which is odd, the graph must cross the $x$-axis at these points. Moreover, because $x=-1,0$ are simple roots, the graph must *not* be tangent to the $x$-axis at these points.\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the root $x=-3$ is $2,$ which is even, the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at this point.\n\nOnly the graphs $\\text{I}$ and $\\text{IV}$ satisfy these conditions.\n\nTo choose between these two graphs, we need to consider the end behavior. Since the leading coefficient is negative, as $x$ approaches $\\infty ,$ $y$ approaches $-\\infty .$\n\nOnly graph $\\text{IV}$ displays this end behavior. Therefore, the correct answer is $\\text{IV}.$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573877 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573826 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573877 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573865 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573877 1069 "q-181990" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573877 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573877 1071 "What are the minimum and maximum of the set $B=\\{9,\\,3,\\,0,\\,7\\}?$\n\n$min(B)=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,max(B)=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573877 1072 #uuid "1f36fa82-e2e0-559a-a43b-3e9b619f4592" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573877 1074 "Recall that \n\n- ${x}_{max}$ is the maximum of a set $S$ if ${x}_{max}\\in S$ and $x\\le {x}_{max}$ for all $x\\in S,$ and\n\n- ${x}_{min}$ is the minimum of a set $S$ if ${x}_{min}\\in S$ and ${x}_{min}\\le x$ for all $x\\in S.$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}min(B) & =min\\{9,\\,3,\\,0,\\,7\\}=0 \\\\ max(B) & =max\\{9,\\,3,\\,0,\\,7\\}=9\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573878 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573794 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573878 1069 "q-102566" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573878 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573878 1071 "If $\\overset{―}{S}=\\{\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{7},\\sqrt{11}\\}$ and $\\overset{―}{T}=\\{\\sqrt{3},\\sqrt{5}\\},$ then what is $\\overset{―}{S\\cup T}?$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573878 1072 #uuid "20b67970-11c0-52b9-9da3-2fda65603f7f" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573878 1074 "De Morgan's law for unions states that\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{S\\cup T}≡\\overset{―}{S}\\cap \\overset{―}{T}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{S\\cup T} & =\\overset{―}{S}\\cap \\overset{―}{T} \\\\  & =\\{\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{7},\\sqrt{11}\\}\\cap \\{\\sqrt{3},\\sqrt{5}\\} \\\\  & =∅.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573879 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573791 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573879 1069 "q-48950" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573879 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573879 1071 "![](images/39/39431059174ad545642732d57f40689e13af962ed2bc091b86eb0d4f41717299.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(2)$ where $h(x)=-2f(x)+3g(x).$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573879 1072 #uuid "2177aae9-0cdc-5d1b-af35-640b6cd8ac62" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573879 1074 "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=-2{f}^{′}(x)+3{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(2)=-2{f}^{′}(2)+3{g}^{′}(2).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=2$ corresponds to \n\n- the interval $x\\in (1,3)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (1,3)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/33/33082034fa45d3228168431e8263c5a9f2c557ce26a8d637596a1e4e6d47d314.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (1,3)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{2-1}{3-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (1,3)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{3-(-1)}{3-1}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(2)=2.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & =-2{f}^{′}(2)+3{g}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573880 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573773 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573880 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573807 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573880 1069 "q-181992" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573880 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573880 1071 "What are the minimum and maximum of the set $C=\\{-7,\\,-1,\\,-4,\\,-5\\}?$\n\n$min(C)=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,max(C)=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573880 1072 #uuid "26063449-9909-57c8-a81e-1b4914486c12" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573880 1074 "Recall that \n\n- ${x}_{max}$ is the maximum of a set $S$ if ${x}_{max}\\in S$ and $x\\le {x}_{max}$ for all $x\\in S,$ and\n\n- ${x}_{min}$ is the minimum of a set $S$ if ${x}_{min}\\in S$ and ${x}_{min}\\le x$ for all $x\\in S.$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}min(C) & =min\\{-7,\\,-1,\\,-4,\\,-5\\}=-7 \\\\ max(C) & =max\\{-7,\\,-1,\\,-4,\\,-5\\}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573881 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573789 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573881 1069 "q-182005" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573881 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573881 1071 "Complete the following reasoning about the set $S=(-\\infty ,-2):$\n\nThe value $-2$ is not the maximum of the set $S$ because {{field-1}}." 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573881 1072 #uuid "2b1de8af-4757-54e2-a638-9811d61ed9c2" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573881 1074 "Recall that ${x}_{max}$ is the maximum of a set $S$ if \n\n- ${x}_{max}\\in S,$ and\n\n- $x\\le {x}_{max}$ for all $x\\in S.$\n\nIn our case, the value $-2$ is not the maximum of the set $S$ because $-2\\notin S.$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573882 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573793 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573882 1069 "q-99288" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573882 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573882 1071 "Given that $p(x)=2x$ and $q(x)=7{x}^{4},$ what is ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(p(x)\\cdot q(x))?$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573882 1072 #uuid "2e570ac4-c7f5-5887-a19e-4808c72f9d67" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573882 1074 "First, we multiply the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(p(x)\\cdot q(x)) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2x\\cdot 7{x}^{4}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }((2\\cdot 7){x}^{1+4}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(14\\cdot {x}^{5}) \\\\  & =14\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{5}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{5}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $14$ does not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{5} &={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{5}=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(14{x}^{5})=\\infty .\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573883 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573815 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573883 1069 "q-344916" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573883 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573883 1071 "![](images/f5/f5f9c20bfeb54e816c98e75469f873d242b26143bdab65e56706861d8cbd7384.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(-2)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$\n\n${h}^{′}(-2)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573883 1072 #uuid "44024ffd-d823-5830-bedd-a0c95dfca83c" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573883 1074 "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-2)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(-2)\\cdot g(-2)-f(-2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-2)}{(g(-2))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(-2)=3$ and $g(-2)=2.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-3,-1)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(-2)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-3,-1)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${g}^{′}(-2)=-1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-2) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(-2)\\cdot g(-2)-f(-2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-2)}{(g(-2))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 2-3\\cdot (-1)}{(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573884 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573780 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573884 1069 "q-31285" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573884 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573884 1071 "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{3}.$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573884 1072 #uuid "4cc89078-03c5-53b0-908e-62267303049d" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573884 1074 "Here, we just take the constant out of the derivative and use the result for the derivative of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-{csc}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡x}{3}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573885 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573862 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573885 1069 "q-278085" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573885 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573885 1071 "The $3$rd term of a geometric sequence is $200$, and the common ratio is $\\frac{5}{4}.$ The first term of this sequence to exceed $8\\,000$ is the{{field-1}} th term." 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573885 1072 #uuid "525be19b-f128-5226-ba8b-06889781710d" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573885 1074 "Since we are given that ${a}_{3}=200$ and  $r=\\frac{5}{4}$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{5}{4})}^{3-1} \\\\ 200 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{5}{4})}^{2} \\\\ 200 & =\\frac{25}{16}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =128\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =128\\cdot {(\\frac{5}{4})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $8\\,000.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}128\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n-1} & >8\\,000 \\\\ {(\\frac{5}{4})}^{n-1} & >62.5 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{5}{4})}^{n-1} & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(62.5) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{5}{4}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(62.5) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(62.5)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{5}{4})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(62.5)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{5}{4})}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(62.5)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{5}{4})}+1≈19.531.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $20$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $8\\,000.$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573886 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573778 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573886 1069 "q-13267" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573886 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573886 1071 "A sphere has a diameter of $11\\,\\text{cm}.$ Find the surface area of the sphere." 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573886 1072 #uuid "54bc8ffe-b872-5461-a4c1-81ec168d4213" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573886 1074 "We can calculate the surface area of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nS=4\\pi {r}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting $r=\\frac{11}{2}\\,\\text{cm}$ into the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\nS=4\\pi {(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}=121\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573887 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573825 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573887 1069 "q-278075" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573887 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573887 1071 "What is the first term that is less than $0.000\\,4$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n128,\\,16,\\,2,\\,\\frac{1}{4},\\,…\n$$\n\nThe first term that is less than $0.000\\,4$ is the\n{{field-1}}th term." 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573887 1072 #uuid "59bb5b60-160e-59f4-91ef-a981e2cb8f1e" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573887 1074 "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=128$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{16}{128}=\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =128{(\\frac{1}{8})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.000\\,4.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.000\\,4 \\\\ 128{(\\frac{1}{8})}^{n-1} & <0.000\\,4 \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{8})}^{n-1} & <\\frac{0.000\\,4}{128} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{1}{8})}^{n-1} & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,003\\,125) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{8}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,003\\,125) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,003\\,125)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{8})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,003\\,125)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{8})}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we reversed the inequality sign when divided by $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{8})$ because $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{8})$ is a negative number. \n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,003\\,125)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{8})}+1≈7.096.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $8$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.000\\,4.$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573888 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573841 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573888 1069 "q-105165" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573888 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573888 1071 "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=b,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573888 1072 #uuid "5a605eaf-55a3-544d-8186-239316368511" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573888 1074 "If ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=b$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-8{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2} &=-1 \\\\ -{x}_{2} &=-2 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation we get ${x}_{2}=2$, which we substitute into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2} & =-1 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-8(2) & =-1 \\\\ 3{x}_{1} & =15 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute ${x}_{1}=5$ and ${x}_{2}=2$ into the third equation to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} & =-2 \\\\ -2(5)+4(2) & =-2 \\\\ -2 & =-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a true statement. \n\nSo the solution of the system is ${x}_{1}=5$ and ${x}_{2}=2,$ which means\n\n$$\n5{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}=b.\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=5+2=7.\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573889 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573840 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573889 1069 "q-32654" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573889 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573889 1071 "A sphere has a diameter of $8\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{mm}.$ Find the surface area of the sphere." 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573889 1072 #uuid "5b24908b-3e08-5600-a3ab-060f182d551c" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573889 1074 "We can calculate the surface area of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nS=4\\pi {r}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting $r=\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{2}=4\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{mm}$ into the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\nS=4\\pi {(4\\sqrt{3})}^{2}=192\\pi \\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573890 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573774 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573890 1069 "q-80349" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573890 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573890 1071 "![](images/2c/2ca762ee3bab6480b69edbea41047f6306dfc049d79db9783075a2c5108deb1d.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the equation of the polynomial curve shown above?" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573890 1072 #uuid "5f84a89a-22f6-5da0-a06c-36c39480c95b" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573890 1074 "First, we notice that the function has precisely three distinct roots, $x=-4$, $x=-1$ and $x=1.$\n\nBased on the graph, we can make the following conclusions about the multiplicities of the roots:\n\n- Since the graph passes through $x=-4,$ this root has odd multiplicity, and since the graph is tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=-4,$ the multiplicity must be greater than one.\n\n- Since the graph passes through $x=-1,$ this root has odd multiplicity, and since the graph is tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=-1,$ the multiplicity must be greater than one.\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=1,$ this root is also a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\nFrom the given options, the only possible equation is $y=(x+4)^{3}(x+1)^{3}(x-1).$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573891 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573838 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573891 1069 "q-108139" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573891 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573891 1071 "The surface area of a sphere is $144\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}.$ Find the volume of the sphere." 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573891 1072 #uuid "63831384-20c1-5413-ac5c-3dae6cac797c" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573891 1074 "We can calculate the surface area of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nS=4\\pi {r}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting $S=144\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}$ into the above formula, we can solve for $r$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 144\\pi  & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{144\\pi }{4\\pi } & ={r}^{2} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =36 \\\\ r & =6\\,\\text{in}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we  can find the volume of the sphere as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi (6)^{3} \\\\  & =288\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573892 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573846 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573892 1069 "q-45064" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573892 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573892 1071 "What is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of the sequence\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=f(n)-\\frac{3}{4},\\,\\,f(1)=-\\frac{5}{4},\\,\\,n\\ge 1?\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573892 1072 #uuid "69a646b1-c870-5cd4-b54f-601f21d9c0db" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573892 1074 "The explicit formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence (in function notation) is\n\n$$\nf(n)=f(1)+(n-1)d,\\,n\\ge 1,\n$$\n\nso we need to determine the first term $f(1)$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nWe are given that the first term is $f(1)=-\\frac{5}{4},$ and we can use the recursive rule to compute the second term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =f(1)-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the first and second terms, we can compute the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =f(2)-f(1) \\\\  & =-2-(-\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $f(1)=-\\frac{5}{4}$ and $d=-\\frac{3}{4}$ into the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =f(1)+(n-1)d \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}+(n-1)(-\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{3}{4}n+\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}n-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\nf(n)=-\\frac{3}{4}n-\\frac{1}{2},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573893 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573821 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573893 1069 "q-37341" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573893 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573893 1071 "Evaluate the gradient of $g(x,y)$ at the point $({e}^{2},1)$ if $g(x,y)=(x+y)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2}).$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573893 1072 #uuid "6b4c3f21-cba5-5b1e-bb44-0204d0b1fcfa" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573893 1074 "First, we find the partial derivatives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x+y)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2})+(x+y)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2}+1+\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x+y)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2})+(x+y)\\cdot (-2y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2}-2xy-2{y}^{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-3{y}^{2}-2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),\\,{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2}+1+\\frac{y}{x},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-3{y}^{2}-2xy⟩ \\\\ ∇g({e}^{2},1) & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2})-{1}^{2}+1+\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2})-3\\cdot {1}^{2}-2\\cdot {e}^{2}\\cdot 1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2+{e}^{-2},-1-2{e}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573894 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573770 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573894 1069 "q-340610" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573894 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573894 1071 "Find the derivative of $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x).$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}\n$)$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573894 1072 #uuid "70b7e2f2-9311-56ba-8d2d-9b32371b2429" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573894 1074 "We calculate the derivative using the product rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x))\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573895 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573803 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573895 1069 "q-113858" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573895 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573895 1071 "$\\int \\frac{\\pi }{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573895 1072 #uuid "7338807d-a806-5642-ae5c-0fdf182692d4" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573895 1074 "Factoring out the constant $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and calculating the resulting integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\pi }{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}{e}^{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573896 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573842 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573896 1069 "q-49065" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573896 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573896 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{3x}.$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573896 1072 #uuid "73a6be96-270e-560a-af2f-74b977e32a10" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573896 1074 "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $\\frac{1}{3}$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{3x} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573897 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573835 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573897 1069 "q-17250" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573897 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573897 1071 "Given that $f(x)=2\\cdot {3}^{x}$,  find the slope of the tangent at $x=1.$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573897 1072 #uuid "75343968-4357-560c-bdef-83ca2d136bed" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573897 1074 "Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\cdot {3}^{x}) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({3}^{x}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot {3}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n                    \n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot {3}^{x}{|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot {3}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573898 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573814 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573898 1069 "q-344801" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573898 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573898 1071 "![](images/34/34d404457497fea78bd8b4e03d9ab9f4cc270487f1c478cb52780748e52163d5.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(1)$ where $h(x)=f(x)+g(x).$\n\n${h}^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573898 1072 #uuid "7b5f58b1-1ad2-5dc4-9868-fc9b1d7a5b07" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573898 1074 "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)+{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(1)={f}^{′}(1)+{g}^{′}(1).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=1$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-1,2)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-1,2)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/62/62c27964999163f87224059d85e5e6fe07a2e8b343e9fef1c34723bf9fbaaec1.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-1,2)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{1-(-2)}{2-(-1)}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(1)=1.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-1,2)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{-2-1}{2-(-1)}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(1)=-1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(1) & ={f}^{′}(1)+{g}^{′}(1) \\\\  & =1+(-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573899 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573804 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573899 1069 "q-82636" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573899 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573899 1071 "Find the contrapositive of the following statement:\n\n$\\,$ **If** you do not go to work, **then** you do not get your salary." 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573899 1072 #uuid "7e17e456-5cfd-52c7-99bf-b5bb8d3b9d9d" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573899 1074 "In our conditional statement: \n\n- The antecedent is $A:$ You do not go to work.\n\n- The consequent is $B:$ You do not get your salary.\n\nThe contrapositive of a conditional statement $A⇒B$ is given by \n\n$$\n¬B⇒¬A.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the negations of $A$ and $B:$\n\n- $¬A:$ You go to work.\n\n- $¬B:$ You get your salary.\n\nTherefore, the contrapositive is the following: \n\n$\\,$ If you get your salary, then you go to work." 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573900 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573861 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573900 1069 "q-314291" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573900 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573900 1071 "Find the slope $m$ of $y=g(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ if $g(x)=-4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+6.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573900 1072 #uuid "7ecca825-7d5a-5032-8595-2b767ed0d377" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573900 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative using the product rule and sum rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,(-4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{d}{dx}(-4{e}^{x})\\cdot \\operatorname{csc}⁡x+(-4{e}^{x})\\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)+\\frac{d}{dx}(6) \\\\  & =-4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+(-4{e}^{x})(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)+0 \\\\  & =-4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\  & =-4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x(1-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and evaluate the slope $m:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={g}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-4{e}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})(1-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =-4{e}^{\\pi /2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573901 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573786 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573901 1069 "q-128524" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573901 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573901 1071 "What is the gradient of the product $f(x,y,z)h(x,y,z)$ if $f(x,y,z)=xyz$ and $h(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)?$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573901 1072 #uuid "7f1d01a7-3767-50a6-8161-9fa62992d18d" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573901 1074 "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $h(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n∇[fh]=f∇h+h∇f.\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyz)=yz \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xyz)=xz \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xyz)=xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\text{ }k=yz\\text{ }i+xz\\text{ }j+xy\\text{ }k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the partial derivatives of $h(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz))=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz))=\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz))=\\frac{1}{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇h(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂h}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂h}{∂y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂h}{∂z}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{z}\\text{ }k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y,z)h(x,y,z)] & =f(x,y,z)∇h(x,y,z)+h(x,y,z)∇f(x,y,z) \\\\  & =xyz(\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{z}\\text{ }k)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)(yz\\text{ }i+xz\\text{ }j+xy\\text{ }k) \\\\  & =\\frac{xyz}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{xyz}{y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{xyz}{z}\\text{ }k+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)(yz\\text{ }i+xz\\text{ }j+xy\\text{ }k) \\\\  & =yz\\text{ }i+xz\\text{ }j+xy\\text{ }k+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)(yz\\text{ }i+xz\\text{ }j+xy\\text{ }k) \\\\  & =(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz))(yz\\text{ }i+xz\\text{ }j+xy\\text{ }k).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573902 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573777 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573902 1069 "q-168112" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573902 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573902 1071 "Which of the following is a type I region in two-dimensional space?" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573902 1072 #uuid "7fda2de1-9ece-56ec-9887-95ffd8614a09" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573902 1074 "A type I region can be written as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le x\\le b,\\,g(x)\\le y\\le f(x)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nA type I region has the property that a vertical line drawn through the region intersects the region in a single interval only.\n\nAmong the given options, the only type I region is the following.\n\n![](images/fc/fc79eeb10b320beb24a54e3cbbbaf8b539fc0c51cb9f6f8ac8127b6faeca1180.png)" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573903 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573833 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573903 1069 "q-65135" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573903 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573903 1071 "What is the gradient of the product $f(x,y)g(x,y)$ if $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y}$ and $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}?$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573903 1072 #uuid "807b4074-7dcc-59b6-a5c1-37bc5db08161" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573903 1074 "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n∇[fg]=f\\,∇\\,g+g\\,∇\\,f\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the first order partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y})={e}^{x+y} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y})={e}^{x+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\text{ }j={e}^{x+y}\\text{ }i+{e}^{x+y}\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the partial derivatives of $g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\text{ }j=2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the product rule, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)g(x,y)] & =({e}^{x+y})(2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j)+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})({e}^{x+y}\\text{ }i+{e}^{x+y}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =2x{e}^{x+y}\\text{ }i+2y{e}^{x+y}\\text{ }j+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}){e}^{x+y}\\text{ }i+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}){e}^{x+y}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x+{y}^{2}){e}^{x+y}\\text{ }i+({x}^{2}+2y+{y}^{2}){e}^{x+y}\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573904 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573811 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573904 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573829 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573904 1069 "q-182010" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573904 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573904 1071 "Complete the following reasoning about the set $S=(4,\\infty ):$\n\nThe value $100$ is not the maximum of the set $S$ because {{field-1}} and yet {{field-2}}." 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573904 1072 #uuid "85d4ab5c-6305-523b-8b3d-8e3d464e6ce6" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573904 1074 "Recall that ${x}_{max}$ is the maximum of a set $S$ if \n\n- ${x}_{max}\\in S,$ and\n\n- $x\\le {x}_{max}$ for all $x\\in S.$\n\nIn our case, the value $100$ is not the maximum of the set $S$ because, for example, $101\\in S$ and yet $101>100.$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573905 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573772 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573905 1069 "q-102569" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573905 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573905 1071 "If $\\overset{―}{R}=\\{{x}_{3},{x}_{5},{x}_{7},{x}_{11}\\}$ and $\\overset{―}{S}=\\{{x}_{4},{x}_{8},{x}_{13}\\},$ then what is $\\overset{―}{R\\cap S}?$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573905 1072 #uuid "88359742-4516-504b-87c3-64da6e9d5379" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573905 1074 "De Morgan's law for intersections states that\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{R\\cap S}≡\\overset{―}{R}\\cup \\overset{―}{S}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{R\\cap S} & =\\overset{―}{R}\\cup \\overset{―}{S} \\\\  & =\\{{x}_{3},{x}_{5},{x}_{7},{x}_{11}\\}\\cup \\{{x}_{4},{x}_{8},{x}_{13}\\} \\\\  & =\\{{x}_{3},{x}_{4},{x}_{5},{x}_{7},{x}_{8},{x}_{11},{x}_{13}\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573906 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573798 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573906 1069 "q-278076" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573906 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573906 1071 "What is the first term that is less than $1$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n125,\\,75,\\,45,\\,27,\\,…\n$$\n\nThe first term that is less than $1$ is the\n{{field-1}}th term." 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573906 1072 #uuid "8cb20152-3f82-5426-99bf-afc35d2d2b76" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573906 1074 "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=125$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{75}{125}=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =125{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $1.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <1 \\\\ 125{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n-1} & <1 \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n-1} & <\\frac{1}{125} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n-1} & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.008) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{5}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.008) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.008)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.008)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{5})}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we reversed the inequality sign when divided by $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{5})$ because $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{5})$ is a negative number. \n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.008)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{5})}+1≈10.452.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $11$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $1.$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573907 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573823 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573907 1069 "q-270985" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573907 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573907 1071 "What is the first term that exceeds  $2\\,000\\,000$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n\\,\\,\\,\\,2,\\,2\\sqrt{5},\\,10,\\,10\\sqrt{5},\\,…\n$$\n\nThe first term that exceeds $2\\,000\\,000$ is the\n{{field-1}}th term." 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573907 1072 #uuid "a4a0b4ff-96f6-5f22-9a8c-2470e817fb96" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573907 1074 "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=2$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{2}=\\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {(\\sqrt{5})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {5}^{(n-1)/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $2\\,000\\,000.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & >2\\,000\\,000 \\\\ 2\\cdot {5}^{(n-1)/2} & >2\\,000\\,000 \\\\ {5}^{(n-1)/2} & >1\\,000\\,000 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({5}^{(n-1)/2}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000) \\\\ \\frac{(n-1)}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{log}⁡(5) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000) \\\\ \\frac{(n-1)}{2} & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)} \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}+1≈18.168.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $19$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $2\\,000\\,000.$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573908 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573802 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573908 1069 "q-71569" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573908 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573908 1071 "For the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2},$ find the equation of the level surface $f(x,y,z)=9.$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573908 1072 #uuid "a616ae7d-06cd-5d59-abb1-f0bb3d95b238" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573908 1074 "Recall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=9.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=9$ is the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=9.$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573909 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573775 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573909 1069 "q-29840" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573909 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573909 1071 "What is the antiderivative of $(\\frac{10}{x}-\\sqrt[3]{x})?$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573909 1072 #uuid "a8ac5ab9-a2e7-5a8d-9104-00d51ece29df" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573909 1074 "First, we apply the sum and constant factor rules and take the coefficients out of the integral. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{10}{x}-\\sqrt[3]{x})\\text{d}x & =10\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int {x}^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\frac{{x}^{1/3+1}}{(\\frac{1}{3}+1)}+C \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{4/3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=10\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $10\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{10}{x}-\\sqrt[3]{x})\\text{d}x & =10\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}+10\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =10(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K)-\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|x|)-\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573910 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573782 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573910 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573796 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573910 1069 "q-182012" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573910 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573910 1071 "Complete the following reasoning about the set $S=(-3,\\infty ):$\n\nThe value $20$ is not the maximum of the set $S$ because {{field-1}} and yet {{field-2}}." 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573910 1072 #uuid "a984b66c-46ea-571e-8eaa-d52949ac9217" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573910 1074 "Recall that ${x}_{max}$ is the maximum of a set $S$ if \n\n- ${x}_{max}\\in S,$ and\n\n- $x\\le {x}_{max}$ for all $x\\in S.$\n\nIn our case, the value $20$ is not the maximum of the set $S$ because, for example, $21\\in S$ and yet $21>20.$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573911 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573809 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573911 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573832 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573911 1069 "q-181998" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573911 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573911 1071 "What are the minimum and maximum of the set $C=[\\sqrt{2},7]?$\n\n$min(C)=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,max(C)=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573911 1072 #uuid "a9a2e605-acfc-58ba-bd69-782902edbf8d" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573911 1074 "Recall that \n\n- ${x}_{max}$ is the maximum of a set $S$ if ${x}_{max}\\in S$ and $x\\le {x}_{max}$ for all $x\\in S,$ and\n\n- ${x}_{min}$ is the minimum of a set $S$ if ${x}_{min}\\in S$ and ${x}_{min}\\le x$ for all $x\\in S.$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}min(C) & =min[\\sqrt{2},7]=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ max(C) & =max[\\sqrt{2},7]=7\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573912 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573820 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573912 1069 "q-277938" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573912 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573912 1071 "What is the first term that exceeds  $1\\,000$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2},\\,2,\\,2\\sqrt{2},\\,4,\\,…\n$$\n\nThe first term that exceeds $1\\,000$ is the\n{{field-1}}th term." 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573912 1072 #uuid "ac65bd0d-2582-576c-9ae6-671d41f53032" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573912 1074 "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=\\sqrt{2}$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot {(\\sqrt{2})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={2}^{n/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $1\\,000.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & >1\\,000 \\\\ {2}^{n/2} & >1\\,000 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({2}^{n/2}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000) \\\\ \\frac{n}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{log}⁡(2) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000) \\\\ \\frac{n}{2} & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}≈19.932.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $20$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $1\\,000.$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573913 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573783 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573913 1069 "q-71570" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573913 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573913 1071 "For the function $f(x,y,z)=12{z}^{2}-3{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2},$ find the equation of the level surface $f(x,y,z)=12.$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573913 1072 #uuid "b089fead-c3aa-5630-9094-3b342b5bd931" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573913 1074 "Recall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=12.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ 12{z}^{2}-3{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}-{z}^{2} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=12$ is the hyperboloid of two sheets $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}-{z}^{2}=-1.$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573914 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573844 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573914 1069 "q-98659" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573914 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573914 1071 "What is the range of $f(x)=-4\\sqrt[6]{4-3x}+2?$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573914 1072 #uuid "b3dce8b7-4368-5263-9706-4359213faf4b" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573914 1074 "The range of $\\sqrt[6]{x}$ is\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[6]{x}\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches do not affect the range. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[6]{4-3x}\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-4$ and then adding $2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[6]{4-3x} & \\ge 0 \\\\ -4\\sqrt[6]{4-3x} & \\le 0 \\\\ -4\\sqrt[6]{4-3x}+2 & \\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,2].$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/03/03804e230140549dd67834281d98130a0113122ded9d491b331d7ae76c621e93.png)" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573915 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573855 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573915 1069 "q-39650" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573915 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573915 1071 "What is the first term that is less than $0.000\\,3$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n125,\\,50,\\,20,\\,8,\\,…\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573915 1072 #uuid "b67cf463-2810-5439-8050-fefe332f47c4" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573915 1074 "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=125$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{50}{125}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =125{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.000\\,3.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.000\\,3 \\\\ 125{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{n-1} & <0.000\\,3 \\\\ {(\\frac{2}{5})}^{n-1} & <\\frac{0.000\\,3}{125} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{n-1} & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,002\\,4) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{5}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,002\\,4) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,002\\,4)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,002\\,4)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{5})}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we reversed the inequality sign when divided by $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{5})$ because $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{5})$ is a negative number. \n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,002\\,4)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{2}{5})}+1≈15.122\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $16$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.000\\,3.$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573916 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573785 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573916 1069 "q-107931" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573916 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573916 1071 "A sphere has a surface area of $48\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}.$ Calculate the diameter of the sphere." 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573916 1072 #uuid "b71e39cd-1ccd-551f-bcc4-e9f7fe121c87" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573916 1074 "We can calculate the surface area of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nS=4\\pi {r}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting $S=48\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}$ into the formula, we can solve for $r$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 48\\pi  & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{48\\pi }{4\\pi } & ={r}^{2} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =12 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{in}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the diameter is\n\n$$\nd=2r=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}=4\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{in}.\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573917 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573853 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573917 1069 "q-288807" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573917 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573917 1071 "Consider the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-83x+1\\,720=0.\n$$\n\n**Without** solving the equation, determine the sum of its solutions." 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573917 1072 #uuid "b7cd0468-ad38-596e-ad84-07e63c57d66b" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573917 1074 "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nFor the given quadratic equation, we have\n\n$$\na=1,\\,b=-83,\\,c=1\\,720.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{b}{a} & =-\\frac{(-83)}{1}=83.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573918 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573863 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573918 1069 "q-106680" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573918 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573918 1071 "Find the inverse of the following statement: \n\n$\\,$ **If** it doesn't rain, **then** Robert goes for a walk." 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573918 1072 #uuid "bc18945c-2d46-5692-9a78-27019da5591c" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573918 1074 "In our conditional statement:\n\n- The antecedent is $A:$ It doesn't rain.\n\n- The consequent is $B:$ Robert goes for a walk.\n\nThe inverse of a conditional statement $A⇒B$ is given by \n\n$$\n¬A⇒¬B.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the negations of $A$ and $B:$\n\n- $¬A:$ It rains.\n\n- $¬B:$ Robert doesn't go for a walk.\n\nTherefore, the inverse is the following: \n\n$\\,$ If it rains, then Robert doesn't go for a walk." 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573919 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573781 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573919 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573848 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573919 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573851 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573919 1069 "q-182000" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573919 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573919 1071 "Complete the following reasoning about the set $S=(-\\infty ,-3]:$\n\nThe value $-3$ is the {{field-1}} of the set $S$ because {{field-2}} and {{field-3}} for all $x\\in S.$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573919 1072 #uuid "bdef5f77-5821-51da-bc48-468fc602838b" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573919 1074 "Recall that ${x}_{max}$ is the maximum of a set $S$ if \n\n- ${x}_{max}\\in S,$ and\n\n- $x\\le {x}_{max}$ for all $x\\in S.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $-3$ is the $\\text{maximum}$ of the set $S$ because\n\n- $-3\\in S,$ and\n\n- $x\\le -3$ for all $x\\in S.$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573920 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573837 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573920 1069 "q-105501" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573920 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573920 1071 "![](images/a8/a8345270e511644cfb019099b907ecadff056f7efbd396e84d4313f3c3ab5817.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(3)$ where $h(x)=f(x)-2g(x).$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573920 1072 #uuid "c089daaf-37cc-52fb-a2a0-6074ff898dc3" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573920 1074 "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)-2{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(3)={f}^{′}(3)-2{g}^{′}(3).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=3$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in (2,4)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (0,4)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/09/09e4836acd54b814c1691c60003614d206fc2eee5cda6d7ad8d6222dd72d2d4f.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (2,4)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{-2-2}{4-2}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(3)=-2.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (0,4)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{-2-0}{4-0}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(3)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(3) & ={f}^{′}(3)-2{g}^{′}(3) \\\\  & =-2-2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573921 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573810 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573921 1069 "q-115847" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573921 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573921 1071 "What is the range of $f(x)=-3\\sqrt[4]{2x-7}+2?$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573921 1072 #uuid "c0ea79a6-c67e-5025-8851-d3340ce04b62" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573921 1074 "The range of $\\sqrt[4]{x}$ is\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[4]{x}\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches do not affect the range. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[4]{2x-7}\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-3$ and then adding $2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[4]{2x-7} & \\ge 0 \\\\ -3\\sqrt[4]{2x-7} & \\le 0 \\\\ -3\\sqrt[4]{2x-7}+2 & \\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,2].$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/e3/e39c45712a29b46df780efe37214aaafb3cb7ab1fa25072c4d9ca21a8bf6c731.png)" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573922 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573864 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573922 1069 "q-49101" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573922 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573922 1071 "What is the antiderivative of ${(1-\\frac{2}{x})}^{2}?$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573922 1072 #uuid "c14c2cc2-7d97-5dae-839b-1375800e6971" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573922 1074 "Expanding the binomial and applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1-\\frac{2}{x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int (1-\\frac{4}{x}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{4}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x-4\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+4\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+4\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{-2+1}}{-2+1}+C \\\\  & =x-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\frac{4}{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1-\\frac{2}{x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =x-\\frac{4}{x}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =x-\\frac{4}{x}-4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K) \\\\  & =x-\\frac{4}{x}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573923 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573850 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573923 1069 "q-76087" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573923 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573923 1071 "What is the set of values of $x$ that satisfy $-2\\le 2x-6\\le 4$ or $12x-22<14?$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573923 1072 #uuid "c2564ad9-789a-55f4-bf0e-d5069f16525a" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573923 1074 "We start by solving $-2\\le 2x-6\\le 4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & \\le 2x-6\\le 4 \\\\ 4 & \\le 2x\\le 10 \\\\ 2 & \\le x\\le 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/c8/c82330c4ccd9aff01ac73cd1ebac56d50f685e0da258fb83ec619ebd11023694.png)\n\nThen, we solve $12x-22<14:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-22 & <14 \\\\ 12x & <36 \\\\ x & <3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/8a/8ade78ec1b4adac7ee63d1eb3e5b5cc82ffe5b00437add005b7824dc2e0c05a9.png)\n\nTo graph the values of $x$ that satisfy either inequality, we overlay the two graphs on the same number line:\n\n![](images/72/72edfbf24542f84652d24d879c2298cff603f960a22885e0b8856635a80d201c.png)\n\nTherefore, the final solution is $x\\le 5.$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573924 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573824 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573924 1069 "q-344908" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573924 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573924 1071 "![](images/c4/c4fd9b46fdb02c23e04c50e437076d7a74f4823546b6608dbe537defb8a439fc.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(-2)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$\n\n${h}^{′}(-2)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573924 1072 #uuid "c31fc413-2779-5fad-acf9-ef1563b305fb" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573924 1074 "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-2)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(-2)\\cdot g(-2)-f(-2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-2)}{(g(-2))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(-2)=1$ and $g(-2)=2.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-3,-1)$ equals $0.$ So, ${f}^{′}(-2)=0.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-3,1)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${g}^{′}(-2)=-1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-2) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(-2)\\cdot g(-2)-f(-2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-2)}{(g(-2))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot 2-1\\cdot (-1)}{(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573925 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573792 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573925 1069 "q-118434" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573925 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573925 1071 "A sphere has a surface area of $25\\pi \\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}.$ Calculate the diameter of the sphere." 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573925 1072 #uuid "c989d950-e23f-561f-b2b4-ddaf69734abf" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573925 1074 "We can calculate the surface area of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nS=4\\pi {r}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting $S=25\\pi \\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}$ into the formula, we can solve for $r$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 25\\pi  & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{25\\pi }{4\\pi } & ={r}^{2} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\,\\text{mm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the diameter is\n\n$$\nd=2r=2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}=5\\,\\text{mm}.\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573926 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573788 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573926 1069 "q-74011" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573926 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573926 1071 "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573926 1072 #uuid "cc7e6aad-0683-5027-b3d4-db532b43dae8" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573926 1074 "If ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ -{x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ -3{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} \\\\ -{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} \\\\ -{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ 0 &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nHowever, the final equation $0=2$  gives a false statement. So the system has no solution." 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573927 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573852 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573927 1069 "q-104314" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573927 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573927 1071 "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)=\\frac{5-2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}.$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573927 1072 #uuid "cdd054fb-7789-52a0-9a24-44d711412a5f" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573927 1074 "We calculate the derivative using the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5-2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x){e}^{x}-(5-2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x){e}^{x}}{({e}^{x})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}(2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-5+2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-5}{{e}^{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573928 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573819 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573928 1069 "q-128528" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573928 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573928 1071 "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y)h(x,y),$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}),\\, &  & ∇f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j, &  &  \\\\ h(x,y) & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\, &  & ∇h(x,y)=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,j. &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573928 1072 #uuid "ce288280-35f0-55c7-872a-791cfea323e8" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573928 1074 "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $h(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n∇[fh]=h∇f+f∇h.\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)h(x,y)] & =h(x,y)∇f(x,y)+f(x,y)∇h(x,y) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j)+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i-\\frac{x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j+\\frac{x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{y\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,i-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,j+\\frac{x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{y\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{y+x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{y\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})-x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}((y+x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}))i+(y\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})-x)j).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573929 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573806 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573929 1069 "q-45073" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573929 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573929 1071 "The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is $f(n)=2n-1$ for $n\\ge 1.$  What is the recursive formula for this sequence?" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573929 1072 #uuid "d00ab483-f4c8-5391-8669-509347c93245" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573929 1074 "The recursive formula for an arithmetic sequence is as follows:\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=f(n)+d,\\,f(1)=___\\,,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo complete the formula, we need to work out the first term and common difference. The first two terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =2(1)-1=1 \\\\ f(2) & =2(2)-1=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the common difference is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =f(2)-f(1) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first term is $f(1)=1$ and the common difference is $d=2.$ Therefore, the recursive formula is as follows:\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=f(n)+2,\\,f(1)=1,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573930 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573784 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573930 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573795 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573930 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573799 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573930 1069 "q-182028" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573930 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573930 1071 "What are the minimum and maximum of the set ${R}_{0}^{+}?$\n\nThe set has no {{field-1}}, while the {{field-2}} is {{field-3}}." 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573930 1072 #uuid "d48b22dd-e8e8-5f04-a9c3-6632dc075c87" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573930 1074 "Recall that \n\n- ${x}_{max}$ is the maximum of a set $S$ if ${x}_{max}\\in S$ and $x\\le {x}_{max}$ for all $x\\in S,$ and\n\n- ${x}_{min}$ is the minimum of a set $S$ if ${x}_{min}\\in S$ and ${x}_{min}\\le x$ for all $x\\in S.$\n\nFirst, recall that, by definition, ${R}_{0}^{+}=[0,\\infty ).$\n\nThis set has $\\text{no maximum},$ while \n\n$$\nmin({R}_{0}^{+})=min[0,\\infty )=0.\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573931 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573827 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573931 1069 "q-108136" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573931 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573931 1071 "![](images/ca/ca021f8684431c29f48876fc2a079f9e23931862f37f8f0dd4b1c2f73a87169d.png)\n\nFind the surface area of the sphere above, given that its volume is $\\frac{9}{2}\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}.$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573931 1072 #uuid "d4951800-9f16-50a9-8666-315cfa908dd9" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573931 1074 "We can find the volume of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting $V=\\frac{9}{2}\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}$ into the formula, we can solve for $r$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{9}{2}\\pi  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{9\\pi \\cdot 3}{2\\cdot 4\\pi } & ={r}^{3} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{27}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{3}}{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{in}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, the surface area of ​​the sphere can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\pi {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =9\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573932 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573847 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573932 1069 "q-74043" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573932 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573932 1071 "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 11 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573932 1072 #uuid "d6a42f32-3ff1-5799-9155-413fd42c36ff" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573932 1074 "If ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 11 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ -5{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{3} \\\\ 11{x}_{3} \\\\ -2{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} \\\\ -5{x}_{2}+11{x}_{3} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ -5{x}_{2}+11{x}_{3} &=-4 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=15\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 11 & -4 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -2 & 15\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 11 & -4 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -2 & 15\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 11 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -14 & -7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -14 & -7 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 11 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{7}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 11 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ {x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=1.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-2(1)=1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=3.\n$$\n\nFinally, by substituting ${x}_{2}=3$ and ${x}_{3}=1$  into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-2(3)+3(1)=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=5.\n$$\n\nSo, the solution of the system is ${x}_{1}=5$, ${x}_{2}=3$, and ${x}_{3}=1,$ which means \n\n$$\n5{a}_{1}+3{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=b.\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=5+3+1=9.\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573933 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573822 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573933 1069 "q-95096" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573933 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573933 1071 "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices given above.\nFind an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables that reduces the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ to the canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy.$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573933 1072 #uuid "d75d5c4d-ca96-55cd-81ad-3a4ef4cad6d7" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573933 1074 "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=0.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573934 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573856 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573934 1069 "q-302063" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573934 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573934 1071 "If $p(x)=3{x}^{6}$ and $q(x)=4x,$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{{x}^{5}\\cdot q(x)}=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573934 1072 #uuid "d9945bb2-9891-5ac6-bca2-13e5bf3bd964" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573934 1074 "First, let's calculate ${x}^{5}\\cdot q(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{5}\\cdot q(x)={x}^{5}\\cdot (4x)=4{x}^{6}.\n$$\n\nWhen we divide the two functions $p(x)$ and ${x}^{5}\\cdot q(x),$ we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{{x}^{5}\\cdot q(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{6}}{4{x}^{6}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573935 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573801 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573935 1069 "q-278084" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573935 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573935 1071 "The $4$th term of a geometric sequence is $192$, and the common ratio is $\\frac{4}{3}.$ The first term of this sequence to exceed $3\\,078$ is the{{field-1}} th term." 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573935 1072 #uuid "e0ccb719-0526-539f-8346-82d0473506ac" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573935 1074 "Since we are given that ${a}_{4}=192$ and  $r=\\frac{4}{3}$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{4-1} \\\\ 192 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{3} \\\\ 192 & =\\frac{64}{27}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =81\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =81\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $3\\,078.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}81\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n-1} & >3\\,078 \\\\ {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n-1} & >38 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{n-1} & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(38) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(38) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(38)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(38)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(38)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})}+1≈13.644.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $14$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $3\\,078.$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573936 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573816 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573936 1069 "q-39653" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573936 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573936 1071 "The $6$th term of a geometric sequence is $\\frac{1}{4}$, and the common ratio is $\\frac{1}{2}.$ What is the first term of this sequence to be less than\n$0.001\\,2?$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573936 1072 #uuid "e0f0f519-39b1-5bf5-a763-c3c4f5d95f00" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573936 1074 "Since we are given that ${a}_{6}=\\frac{1}{4}$ and  $r=\\frac{1}{2}$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{6} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{6-1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{2}^{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{32}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =8\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={2}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{1-n} \\\\  & ={2}^{4-n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.001\\,2.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{4-n} & <0.001\\,2 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(2)^{4-n} & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.001\\,2) \\\\ (4-n)\\operatorname{log}⁡(2) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.001\\,2) \\\\ 4-n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.001\\,2)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)} \\\\ -n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.001\\,2)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}-4 \\\\ n & >4-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.001\\,2)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n4-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.001\\,2)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}≈13.703\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $14$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.001\\,2.$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573937 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573812 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573937 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573817 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573937 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573849 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573937 1069 "q-182025" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573937 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573937 1071 "What are the minimum and maximum of the set $A=\\{4,\\,1,\\,-2,\\,-5,\\,…\\}?$\n\nThe set has no {{field-1}}, while the {{field-2}} is {{field-3}}." 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573937 1072 #uuid "e1939d8b-89f3-56f8-a87f-ff6f33d4d727" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573937 1074 "Recall that \n\n- ${x}_{max}$ is the maximum of a set $S$ if ${x}_{max}\\in S$ and $x\\le {x}_{max}$ for all $x\\in S,$ and\n\n- ${x}_{min}$ is the minimum of a set $S$ if ${x}_{min}\\in S$ and ${x}_{min}\\le x$ for all $x\\in S.$\n\nNotice that elements of $A$ form an infinite, decreasing arithmetic sequence. \n\nTherefore, this set has $\\text{no minimum},$ while \n\n$$\nmax(A)=max\\{4,\\,1,\\,-2,\\,-5,\\,…\\}=4.\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573938 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573818 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573938 1069 "q-128518" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573938 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573938 1071 "Given that $h(x,y)=({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}){e}^{x}$, find the points $(x,y)$ where $∇h(x,y)=0.$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573938 1072 #uuid "e83d5b90-8fee-5f1e-85b9-d81d6b0e4f2a" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573938 1074 "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}){e}^{x}) \\\\  & =2x{e}^{x}+({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}){e}^{x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}){e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}){e}^{x}) \\\\  & =-2y{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇h(x,y)=({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}){e}^{x}\\,i-2y{e}^{x}\\,j.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇h(x,y)=0:$\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}){e}^{x}\\,i-2y{e}^{x}\\,j=0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$ and $j,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}){e}^{x} &=0, \\\\ -2y{e}^{x} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $y=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+2x-{0}^{2}){e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ x(x+2){e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $∇h(x,y)=0$ at two points: $(-2,0)$ and $(0,0).$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573939 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573839 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573939 1069 "q-25487" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573939 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573939 1071 "Which of the following number lines represents the values of $w$ that satisfy $3w-7\\le 5$ or $5w+7\\ge -3$, or $2w<8?$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573939 1072 #uuid "e93fe40f-a4aa-5acf-8d5c-2ac8fa4691b8" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573939 1074 "We start by solving $3w-7\\le 5:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3w-7 & \\le 5 \\\\ 3w & \\le 12 \\\\ w & \\le 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/df/dfbf9fce10352e640bb62b75c49339e54c3d22b918e2dfb60a72472ef5129639.png)\n\nThen, we solve $5w+7\\ge -3:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5w+7 & \\ge -3 \\\\ 5w & \\ge -10 \\\\ w & \\ge -2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/53/535934975bf21706e8bfa3b54fa51a0f344f585e89f3003ad042f6fb660c3441.png)\n\nFinally, we solve $2w<8:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2w & <8 \\\\ w & <4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/ba/ba806ca20f496c724c4411c282ab44d81d7713c7b8635b3c286faff1d7728f20.png)\n\nTo graph the values of $w$ that satisfy at least one of the inequalities, we overlay the three graphs on the same number line:\n\n![](images/f0/f084c11e4faf4fdcfc69a32900bf62c1bddfa2266007f8f4ddecf8ab928230fa.png)\n\nTherefore, the final solution is $-\\infty <w<\\infty .$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573940 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573779 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573940 1069 "q-270990" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573940 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573940 1071 "What is the first term that is less than $0.000\\,000\\,5$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n\\,\\,\\,\\,256,\\,64,\\,16,\\,4,\\,…\n$$\n\nThe first term that is less than $0.000\\,000\\,5$ is the\n{{field-1}}th term." 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573940 1072 #uuid "ebe8e960-0d95-59ae-8282-f9852d97bd86" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573940 1074 "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=256$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{64}{256}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =256\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={4}^{4}\\cdot {4}^{1-n} \\\\  & ={4}^{5-n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.000\\,000\\,5.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.000\\,000\\,5 \\\\ {4}^{5-n} & <0.000\\,000\\,5 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({4}^{5-n}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,5) \\\\ (5-n)\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,5) \\\\ 5-n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,5)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)} \\\\ -n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,5)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}-5 \\\\ n & >5-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,5)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n5-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,5)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}≈15.466.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $16$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.000\\,000\\,5.$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573941 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573790 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573941 1069 "q-183294" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573941 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573941 1071 "Find the converse of the following statement: \n\n$\\,$ **If** there are no clouds in the sky, **then** it is not raining." 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573941 1072 #uuid "ecab2b8f-98e3-5f6d-b4a6-b5491b118c68" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573941 1074 "In our conditional statement:\n\n- The antecedent is $A:$ There are no clouds in the sky.\n\n- The consequent is $B:$ It is not raining.\n\nThe converse of a conditional statement $A⇒B$ is given by \n\n$$\nB⇒A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the converse is the following: \n\n$\\,$ If it is not raining, then there are no clouds in the sky." 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573942 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573813 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573942 1069 "q-218497" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573942 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573942 1071 "What is the first term that exceeds $1\\,200\\,000$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n3,\\,3\\sqrt{6},\\,18,\\,18\\sqrt{6},\\,…\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573942 1072 #uuid "f1da87a1-4d7e-5076-b588-28216bd9d666" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573942 1074 "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=3$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{3}=\\sqrt{6}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (\\sqrt{6})^{n-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {6}^{(n-1)/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $1\\,200\\,000.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & >1\\,200\\,000 \\\\ 3\\cdot {6}^{(n-1)/2} & >1\\,200\\,000 \\\\ {6}^{(n-1)/2} & >400\\,000 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({6}^{(n-1)/2}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(400\\,000) \\\\ \\frac{(n-1)}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{log}⁡(6) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(400\\,000) \\\\ \\frac{(n-1)}{2} & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(400\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(6)} \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(400\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(6)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(400\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(6)}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(400\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(6)}+1≈15.398\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $16$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $1\\,200\\,000.$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573943 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573857 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573943 1069 "q-313721" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573943 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573943 1071 "Find ${g}^{′}(x)$ if $g(x)=3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).$\n\n${g}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573943 1072 #uuid "f25a3394-05aa-5c37-ad51-535ad5e51912" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573943 1074 "We take the derivative of each term and then add them, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)) \\\\  & =3(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x))+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573944 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573787 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573944 1069 "q-71910" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573944 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573944 1071 "Calculate $\\int {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573944 1072 #uuid "f3a89c81-1f21-57c4-8472-9cb98515b280" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573944 1074 "To compute the integral of a general exponential function, we just need to divide by the natural logarithm of the base of the exponential function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3})}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the laws of logarithms, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\  & =0-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can simplify our final answer as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3})}+C &=-\\frac{{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573945 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573805 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573945 1069 "q-129191" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573945 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573945 1071 "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{z}{y-x}$ that passes through the point $P(2,1,3).$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573945 1072 #uuid "f4d46c54-bc24-5068-beab-fa23fb4611cc" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573945 1074 "First, we find the value of the function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,1,3) & =\\frac{z}{y-x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{1-2} \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=-3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\frac{z}{y-x} & =-3 \\\\ z & =-3(y-x) \\\\ z & =3x-3y \\\\ 3x-3y-z & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P(2,1,3)$ is the plane $3x-3y-z=0.$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573946 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573776 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573946 1069 "q-71477" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573946 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573946 1071 "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y)g(x,y),$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =x+y,\\, &  & ∇f(x,y)=\\text{ }i+\\text{ }j, &  &  \\\\ g(x,y) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y),\\, &  & ∇g(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x+y}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{x+y}\\text{ }j. &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573946 1072 #uuid "f7be3188-95a1-5bd7-a100-6bc1c7138029" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573946 1074 "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n∇[fg]=g\\,∇\\,f+f\\,∇\\,g\n$$\n\nSo, using this product rule we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)g(x,y)] & =g(x,y)∇f(x,y)+f(x,y)∇g(x,y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)(\\text{ }i+\\text{ }j)+(x+y)(\\frac{1}{x+y}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{x+y}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\text{ }i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\text{ }j+\\frac{x+y}{x+y}\\text{ }i+\\frac{x+y}{x+y}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\text{ }i+\\text{ }i)+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\text{ }j+1\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1)\\text{ }i+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1)\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573947 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573836 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573947 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573843 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573947 1069 "q-181725" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573947 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573947 1071 "What are the minimum and maximum of the set $A=(2,5]?$\n\nThe minimum of the set {{field-1}}, while the maximum of the set {{field-2}}." 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573947 1072 #uuid "fd347120-7271-5332-88f3-ec596dd93ff3" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573947 1074 "Recall that \n\n- ${x}_{max}$ is the maximum of a set $S$ if ${x}_{max}\\in S$ and $x\\le {x}_{max}$ for all $x\\in S,$ and\n\n- ${x}_{min}$ is the minimum of a set $S$ if ${x}_{min}\\in S$ and ${x}_{min}\\le x$ for all $x\\in S.$\n\nNotice that $2\\notin A.$ So, we have the following:\n\n- $min(A)=min(2,5],$ which $\\text{does not exist}$\n\n- $max(A)=max(2,5]=5$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573948 1068 #edb/ref 17592186573808 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573948 1069 "q-140438" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573948 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573948 1071 "Given that $f(x)={5}^{x}+2{e}^{x}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$,  find the slope of the tangent at $x=1.$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573948 1072 #uuid "fe3ddee2-6465-59d2-8bfd-072b67b0281b" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573948 1074 "Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({5}^{x}+2{e}^{x}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({5}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & ={5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & ={5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+2{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & =({5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+2{e}^{x}){|}_{x=1} \\\\  & ={5}^{1}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡5+2\\cdot {e}^{1} \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+2e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573949 1062 #uuid "04ddc6fd-b21b-52ad-8cd2-7aed3b7a2fb9" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573949 1063 #edb/ref 17592186364558 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573950 1058 #edb/ref 17592186573968 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573950